    "0b6c1a10-0000-4000-8001-000000000000": {
      "name": "The 26 August Nepal flood, cause and course",
      "parameters": {
        "content": {
          "attachments": {},
          "contentMode": "markdown",
          "image": "",
          "imageCaption": "",
          "markdown": "## The 26 August Nepal flood, cause and course\n\n**On 26 August 2026 a seismometer in Nepal recorded what looked like a\nmagnitude 5.2 earthquake. It was not one. It was a flood starting.**\n\nAt **02:52 UTC**, 08:37 in the morning in Nepal, ice and rock collapsed high on\nthe Langtang range. The water it released ran **210 km**\ndown four rivers in turn: 10 km of Lhende Khola, 14 km of Bhote Koshi, 164 km of Trishuli, 21 km of Narayani.\n\nThe event is named for the Bhote Koshi, which is where it reached a major river.\nMost of what this map shows is the Trishuli.\n\nIt reached the first town in about twenty minutes, and the Ganga the next day. That difference is the warning time, and the second half of this story is about how it grows.\n\nThe story follows the seismic signal first, then follows the water. Everything in it comes from public data: the earthquake catalogue, one seismometer, the terrain, OpenStreetMap, and reporting from the days after.\n\nEvery segment that shows the map ends with a key for the marks on it. Every number is either measured by the scripts or linked to where it came from.\n\n---\n\n\n**This map does not carry the human toll.** Nepal's\n[NDRRMA](https://bipadportal.gov.np/) publishes the casualty and response figures.\n\n*Scroll to begin.*\n\n\n---",
          "paneAlignment": "start",
          "panelWidth": "34%",
          "title": "A flood that was first reported as an earthquake"
        },
        "extent": [
          9256882.117232194,
          3081345.6937492713,
          9711959.114141138,
          3416601.8856210187
        ],
        "layerOverride": [
          {
            "opacity": 0.4,
            "targetLayer": "0b6c1a10-0000-4000-8000-000000000002",
            "visible": true
          },
          {
            "opacity": 1.0,
