    "0b6c1a10-0000-4000-8001-000000000015": {
      "name": "How wide the valley is at three places",
      "parameters": {
        "content": {
          "attachments": {
            "valley-cross-sections.png": {
              "image/png": ""
            }
          },
          "contentMode": "markdown",
          "image": "",
          "imageCaption": "",
          "markdown": "## How wide the valley is at three places\n\n**The valley floor is 75 m wide at\nTimure, 180 m at Galchhi and\n135 m at Malekhu.**\n\n![The valley cut across at three places](attachment:valley-cross-sections.png)\n\nEach panel is a slice across the valley, the way a slice of bread cuts a loaf.\nZero is the lowest point: the riverbed. The higher the walls climb on either\nside, the less room the water has.\n\nThe blue lines are river levels reported by ICIMOD [after the\nflood](https://www.icimod.org/press-release/major-flash-flood-sweeps-through-nepals-rasuwa-district-raising-fears-of-further-downstream-flooding/): **+9 m in half an\nhour** at Galchhi, **+7 m** at Malekhu.\n\n**One number in each panel is a picture, not a measurement.** The width beside\nthe blue line is that reported level drawn against a coarse elevation model from\nthe year 2000. It shows how much room the valley offers a rise like that. It\ndoes not show where the water actually went; nobody had published that yet.\n",
          "paneAlignment": "start",
          "panelWidth": "34%",
          "title": "How wide the valley is at three places"
        },
        "extent": [
          9370369.77508147,
          3166556.179777314,
          9542811.71089464,
          3293594.520788613
        ],
        "layerOverride": [
