    "0b6c1a10-0000-4000-8001-000000000020": {
      "name": "Explore the map yourself",
      "parameters": {
        "content": {
          "attachments": {},
          "contentMode": "markdown",
          "image": "",
          "imageCaption": "",
          "markdown": "## Explore the map yourself\n\n**Everything the story showed you is still in this file, and all of it you can inspect [here](https://notebook.link/@arjxn-py/nff)**\n\n**1. Ask a feature where it came from.** Turn on the identify tool in the\ntoolbar and click any dot or line. Every feature in this example carries its\nsource, the link to that source, its licence and the date it was fetched. A\ntributary also carries the kilometre at which it joins the flood path, a\nwaypoint carries its arrival window and whether that window was measured or\nworked out, and a bridge carries a plain note saying it was exposed rather than\nreported damaged.\n\n**2. Turn layers on and off.** The story only ever showed a few at a time, which\nis a storyteller's choice, not a limit. The layer panel has all of them: the\nflood path and its reaches, the tributaries, the terrain, the distance bands,\nthe places, the cross-sections, the bridges, the schools and health posts, the\nvalley road, and the Gandak and Ganga downstream.\n\n**3. Look at how the colours are decided.** Open the symbology for **Reaches:\nhow steep the channel is**. The three colours are a categorical scale with the\nstops written into the file, which is why the legend names each reach. Change a\nstop and the map changes with it.\n\n",
          "paneAlignment": "start",
          "panelWidth": "34%",
          "title": "Explore the map yourself"
        },
